   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the vehicle and traffic law, in relation to the residential parking system in the village of Dobbs Ferry in the county of Westchester; and providing for the repeal of such provisions upon expiration thereof",
    "basePrintNo": "S5187",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S5187-2015",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Add §1640-o, V & T L",
    "lawSection": "Vehicle and Traffic Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S5187                REVISED 5/7/15\n\nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the vehicle and traffic law, in\nrelation to the residential parking system in the village of Dobbs\nFerry in the county of Westchester; and providing for the repeal of\nsuch provisions upon expiration thereof\n\nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:  To authorize a residential parking\nsystem in the village of Dobbs Ferry\n\nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 of the bill describes the Legislative findings and intent.\nThe legislature finds that on street parking in certain residential\ndistricts in the village of Dobbs Ferry are proximate to the Dobbs\nFerry train station and have been unduly burdened by commuters\nlimiting access to the neighborhood residents. The provision of on\nstreet parking permits would allow the residents to park adjacent to\ntheir homes.\n\nSection 2 amends the vehicle and traffic law by adding a new section\n1640-o. By adoption of a local law, the village board of the village\nof Dobbs Ferry may provide a residential parking permit system and fix\nand require fees in accordance with the provisions of the section. A\nresidential parking permit system may only be established on the\nfollowing roadways: westerly side of Broadway, from Oak street to\nLivingston avenue; Oak street, from Broadway to the municipal parking\nlot behind 112 Main street; Elm street, from Broadway to the municipal\nparking lot behind 112 Main street; Chestnut street, from Broadway to\nMain street; Walnut street, from Broadway to Livingston avenue; Hatch\nterrace, from Broadway to Walnut street; Palisade street, from Cedar\nstreet to Chestnut street; Riverside place; Cedar street, from Main\nstreet to a point 750 feet west of Palisade street; and the portion of\nDraper lane owned by the village of Dobbs Ferry.\n\nSection 3 states that no permit shall be required on those portions of\nstreets where the adjacent properties are zoned for commercial, office\nand/or retail use. Section 4 provides what should be included in the\nlocal law. Section 5 provides that a public hearing must be held and\nSection 6 states that the parking system does not apply to any state\nhighway.\n\nJUSTIFICATION:  A residential parking permit is necessary because of\nthe inadequacy of on-street parking for the residents in the area\nlocated near the Dobbs Ferry train station. Residents of the\ndesignated roadways are unable to find parking near their home because\nof the proximity to the train station. There is adequate parking at\nthe train station for commuters.\n\nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY: New Bill\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS: None to the State.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:  This act shall take effect on the sixtieth day after\nit shall have become a law and shall expire on January 1, 2018.\n\n\n",
