    "lawCode": "Add §1640-o, V & T L",
    "lawSection": "Vehicle and Traffic Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S5187A\n\nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the vehicle and traffic law, in\nrelation to the residential parking system in the village of Dobbs\nFerry in the county of Westchester; and providing for the repeal of\nsuch provisions upon expiration thereof\n\nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL: To authorize a residential parking\nsystem in the village of Dobbs Ferry\n\nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 of the bill describes the Legislative findings and intent.\nThe legislature finds that the village of Dobbs Ferry encompasses\napproximately 3 miles, has over 11,000 residents and within its\nboundaries a railroad station maintained and operated by the Metro\nNorth division of the Metropolitan Transportation Authority. The\nstation attracts a large number of commuters and travelers from\noutside the village resulting in significant traffic and congestion.\nAlthough there are public and private off-street parking available,\ncommuters and train passengers park on the local streets adjoining the\nstation. The residential neighborhood adjoining the station lacks\nsufficient off-street parking and the addition of the overflow\ncommuter parking poses a hazard to residents and pedestrians in the\narea.\n\nTherefore, the legislature declares the necessity of this act for the\nvillage of Dobbs Ferry in the county of Westchester to adopt a\nresidential parking system.\n\nSection 2 amends the vehicle and traffic law by adding a new section\n1640-o. By adoption of a local law, the village board of the village\nof Dobbs Ferry may provide a residential parking permit system and fix\nand require fees in accordance with the provisions of the section.\n\nA residential parking permit system may only be established on the\nfollowing roadways: (a) Oak street, from Broadway to Main street; (b)\nElm street, from Broadway to Main street; (c) Chestnut street, from\nBroadway to Main street; (d) Walnut street, from Broadway to\nLivingston avenue; (e) Hatch terrace, from Broadway to Walnut street;\n(f) Palisade street, from Cedar street to Chestnut street; (g)\nRiverside place; (h) Cedar street, from Main street to a point 750\nfeet west of Palisade street; and (i) the portion of Draper lane owned\nby the village of Dobbs Ferry.\n\nSection 2 further states that no permit shall be required on those\nportions of streets where the adjacent properties are zoned for\ncommercial, office and/or retail use. The section provides for what\nshould be included in the local law and provides that a public hearing\nmust be held. The parking system does not apply to any state highway\nmaintained by the state.\n\nSection 3 sets forth the effective date.\n\nJUSTIFICATION: A residential parking permit is necessary because of\nthe inadequacy of on-street parking for the residents in the area\nlocated near the Dobbs Ferry train station. Residents of the\n\n\ndesignated roadways are unable to find parking near their home because\nof the proximity to the train station. There is adequate parking at\nthe train station for commuters and train passengers.\n\nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY: New Bill\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS: None to the State.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE: The act shall take effect on the sixtieth day after it\nshall have become law and shall expire on January 1, 2018 when upon\nsuch date the provisions of this act shall be deemed repealed.\n\n",
